# umap_vs_pca: two-moons embedded in 5-D, projected back to 2-D
# by both PCA (linear) and UMAP (non-linear / manifold-aware).
#
# Setup: take the 2-D two-moons dataset, append three small-
# variance noise dimensions so the data lives in 5-D but its
# intrinsic structure stays 2-D. Both methods then project down
# to 2-D.
#
# Expectation: PCA recovers something close to the original 2-D
# layout because the noise dims are low-variance; UMAP recovers
# the two moons from local neighborhood structure with the a/b
# curve params fitted from min_dist (saga 33 step 037c). Both
# scatters show class 0 / class 1 as visually distinct regions.
M = moons(7, 100, 0.05)                                            # 100 x 3: x, y, label
moons2d = matmul(M, [[1, 0], [0, 1], [0, 0]])                       # drop label column
labels = reshape(matmul(M, [[0], [0], [1]]), [100])                # extract integer labels
embed = randn(42, [100, 3]) * 0.3                                  # 3 noise dims, smaller variance than the moons
X = concat(moons2d, embed, 1)                                      # 100 x 5
# PCA: top 2 linear principal components. Since the moons span
# more variance than the noise dims, PC1/PC2 should grab the
# moons structure.
pca_proj = pca(X, 2)
# UMAP: builds a k-NN graph, fuzzy simplicial set, then SGD
# layout. Local neighborhood structure carries the moons even
# under heavier noise.
umap_proj = umap(X, 15, 0.1, 200, 7)
# Two scatter plots, colored by class. Side by side they show
# "linear" vs "manifold-aware" projection of the same 5-D data.
scatter_labeled(pca_proj, labels)
scatter_labeled(umap_proj, labels)
